fferently on new test samples because  these two are very different functions  that means even if we successfully solve  the optimization problem and get small  training arrow that doesn't imply we  will have generalization we will have  small test error because in principle we  don't know whether we found the blue  solution or the red solution  if we just look at the training training  arrow but in practice  just by minimizing the training loss  using gradient descent-like algorithms  we can somehow often find solutions that  generalize well to test samples and this  is the the mystery that's not well  understood  and what is believed to be an  explanation of this behavior is that  there's an implicit regulation effect  from the optimization algorithms we use  the main proposal is that the  optimization algorithms such as gradient  descent or stochastic gradient descent  prefers low complexity solutions so even  if the whole model space is very complex  gradient design will only find certain  low complexity solutions for some reason  and that if that's true that would imply  a good generalization performance  so i'm gonna present two uh simple deep  learning examples where we are able to  mathematically characterize the implicit  organization going on there so the first  one is for linear networks and the  second is for nonlinear networks  so let me uh go into the first example  so for for this example we will consider  this classical problem of matrix  completion so the goal of measures  completion is to recover a low rank  matrix given only a subset of its  entries  and this is a well-stated problem in  machine learning and statistics and the  typical application is in recommendation  systems so think about predicting user  ratings but given only a subset of the  ratings so today for simplicity we'll  just assume that  this ground choose matrix which i call m  is a square matrix so it's a d by d  matrix and it it has low rank  uh so uh so how do you solve this  problem the first we can easily write  down a naive training loss which looks  like this but we try to find a matrix w  which is uh our model that minimizes  this training loss over the observed  entries  and of course many optima exists for  this problem because we can just assign  any values to the unobserved entries so  there's no hope of recovering the true  matrix m using this naive approach but  that's simply because we haven't used  the assumption that the matrix should be  low rank so we may want to add some  explicit regularization  to encourage low rank and the classic  approach is to add a nuclear norm  regularization like this we recall that  nuclear norm is the sum of all the  singular values of a matrix which can be  viewed as a convex relaxation of the  rank  but what what we are considering today  is a different and a counter-intuitive  approach which i call a deep learning  approach so what we do here is to try to  find this matrix m as a product of  multiple matrices and in the language of  deep learning uh this can be viewed as a  deep linear network because it's  parameterized as a product of l matrices  w1 through wl which is a composition of  l layers of linear transformations so  here's the objective function  corresponding to this approach and so  it's a function of this l matrices w1  through wl and notice that here i'm i'm  requiring all these matrices to have to  be size d by d so we are by doing this  we are not imposing any constraint on  the rank of the matrix and actually it's  very easy to see that this problem is  exactly the same as the problem in this  naive method because we can still  express all the d by d matrices is using  the product of multiple d by d matrices  so it seems that doing this  reprimatization doesn't make much sense  and we wouldn't expect this to be useful  at all  but the but the surprise is that  empirically if we just minimize this new  objective using gradient descent with  initialization close to zero we can  we can almost always recover the ground  choose matrix and accurately  and this is surprising because we are  not explicitly asking for a low rank  solution in the objective  so the only possibility is that the  optimization algorithm we use which is  gradient descent in this case has an  implicit regulation that somehow can  help us find the low rank branches  matrix  and this implicit regulation effect we  can understand it uh by analyzing the  dynamics of the gradient descent  algorithm in this problem and an  important step in the analysis is to  derive the dynamics of the singular  values of the solution matrix which is  this equation and from from this  equation we can see that the depth the  number of layers l affects this dynamics  via this blue vector and we can  interpret it as inducing a rich get  richer phenomenon among different  singular values which encourages low  rank because different singular values  will grow at very different rates  according to this equation and we can  verify this intuition by plotting the  evolution of all the singular values  during the course of gradient descent  okay so basically for this problem we  had a very nice mental picture of how  the gradient descent algorithm prefers  low complexity solutions and the  appropriate notion of complexity here is  the rank of the solution matrix and and  we can divide the space of all the  solutions according to their rank  and our grant use matrix might be here  which is a low rank global minimum of  the loss function but there are also  many other global minima which have  higher rent and they all have zero  training loss  but so what happens uh when we run  gradient descent starting close to zero  is that it will search first search over  the rank one spaces and then the rank  two ranks three and eventually it will  settle down at a low rank minimum so it  will never reach these higher rank  spaces so it won't go to all those  non-generalizable minima and this is  done implicitly by running gradient  descent  so we can view uh this dynamic picture  uh  as indicating a more general statement  that uh maybe neural network learns  functions of increasing complexity  during training and the notion of the  complexity in this example was the rank  of the matrix  and and this general hypothesis is to  believe to be perhaps true  in some sense but in general we still  don't know what is the right way to  characterize the complexity of neural  networks and especially in non-linear  deep neural networks uh this becomes  much more challenging than linear  networks  uh so and next i'm gonna very briefly  talk about another interesting  theoretical result along the lines of  this general hypothesis and in  particular we will show that in some  natural settings a non-linear network  trained by gradient descent only learns  a linear function first before it starts  to learn anything else  and so the formal result is for a  standard two-layer neural network and by  gradient descent so the network looks  like this so it has two layers and  there's non-linearity in the hidden  layer of the network  and we also consider a hypothetical  process  which is a linear model also trained by  gradient descent so uh so it's just a  simple linear model on the input x so  the beta 1 and beta 2 here are the  parameters in this linear model  and what we prove uh is that these two  processes can be coupled and  specifically in the early time of  running gradient descent the neural  network and the linear model compute  almost the same function for input x  coming from the underlying data  distribution  so that means although the neural  network has non-linear capacity  uh if we but if when we are running  gradient descent it will implicitly bias  the neural network towards a simple  linear function at the beginning of of  training so this is also a very  interesting implicit realization effect  so here are some experiments to to  verify this result so basically these  plots are showing that uh the neural  network uh and the hypothetical linear  model basically behave the same in the  in the early time of gradient descent  which verifies uh this theorem  okay so so to to conclude uh i argued  that uh the implicit regulation effect  from the optimization algorithms is key  to understanding the generalization  behavior in modern deep learning and i  presented two case studies where we are  able to show that uh show exactly how  neural network learns functions of  increasing complexity during the course  of gradient descent and these examples  also show that uh perhaps analyzing the  dynamics of the learning procedure may  be important to understand or even just  to describe the implicit requisition  effect in deep learning  okay that's the end of my talk and uh  feel free to get in touch if you are  interested in chatting about deep  learning thanks  [Music]  um  we'll move to my no list now so i'll  just tell this in a chat  amanolis do you hear me  [Music]  yep i can hear you can you hear me yes

